## 2. Why NLP-Based Screening?

### 2.1 The Help-Seeking Gap

88.7% of Singapore young adults prefer online over face-to-face help-seeking (MOH, 2024). Current platforms are **reactive** — they serve people who seek them out. Most at-risk adolescents never initiate contact.

**The NLP solution:** Passively monitor digital text where adolescents already express distress naturally — identifying at-risk individuals before crisis.

### 2.2 Advantages Over Traditional Screening

| Approach | Coverage | Frequency | Stigma |
|---|---|---|---|
| Clinical interview | Low | Occasional | High barrier |
| Self-report surveys | Moderate | Termly | Moderate |
| Teacher referral | Low | Ad hoc | High dependency |
| **NLP pipeline** | **High** | **Continuous** | **No barrier** |

---

## 3. Clinical Rationale for 3-Tier Architecture

### 3.1 Why Three Tiers?

A single-model approach creates unacceptable tradeoffs. The 3-tier architecture resolves this:
Tier 1: Cast wide net (high sensitivity, fast, cheap)
↓ Only high-distress cases proceed
Tier 2: Add context and nuance (balanced sensitivity/specificity)
↓ Generate interpretable risk score
Tier 3: Add temporal dimension (identify peak risk windows)
↓ Optimal intervention timing
### 3.2 Tier 1 — MentalBERT Rationale

MentalBERT was pre-trained on Chinese social media mental health text — the same distribution as the target population. Domain specificity is clinically critical:

- General BERT tokenises "绝望感" as separate characters, losing compound meaning
- MentalBERT preserves compound distress terms as semantic units
- Tuned for **high sensitivity** — missing a true at-risk individual is a clinical error

### 3.3 Tier 2 — XGBoost + SHAP Rationale

XGBoost chosen over deep neural networks specifically for **clinical interpretability**. Counsellors must understand *why* an individual was flagged — not just *that* they were flagged.

**Feature selection rationale:**

| Feature | Clinical Basis |
|---|---|
| Hopelessness language | Strongest predictor of suicide risk (Beck, 1974) |
| Social isolation | Key depression symptom; predicts escalation |
| Late-night posting | Disrupted sleep is both symptom and risk factor |
| Academic stress | Singapore-specific; exam periods correlate with crises |
| Somatic complaints | Culturally normative distress proxy in Asian populations |
| Positive language (inverse) | Low positive affect predicts depression onset |
| Code-switching | Higher emotional arousal in bilingual speakers |

### 3.4 Tier 3 — LSTM Rationale

Grounded in Eckstein et al. (2026): human behaviour encodes risk history at multiple timescales. Point-in-time assessment misses:

- **Trend direction:** Score of 65 increasing is more concerning than one decreasing
- **Peak prediction:** Knowing risk peaks in 2 days enables proactive outreach today
- **Pattern recognition:** Cyclical patterns (exam anxiety, weekend isolation)
Point-in-time: "Risk score = 65" → Counsellor uncertain when to act
Trajectory:    "Risk peaks at 78 in 2 days" → Counsellor acts today

